  ---

  [1mSpec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-16-task-list-core-design.md[0m (not committed). Please review it and let
  me know if you want any changes before I write the implementation plan.

  Where things landed, in short:

  Your request was a notification system. The repo turned out to be an empty HTML page — no tasks, no users, no storage
  — so there was nothing to notify about. We decomposed into [1mtask list core[0m (this spec) and [1mnotifications[0m (a second
  spec, written later against real code), and you chose to build them in that order.

  The spec covers a single-user, browser-only, zero-dependency task list: a [38;5;153m{id, title, done, dueAt, createdAt, [39m
  [38;5;153mupdatedAt}[39m model, a store/storage/view split with one-way data flow, and [38;5;153mnode --test[39m unit tests on the pure-logic
  modules.

  The part worth your attention is the [1mInterface Contract for Sub-Project 2[0m section. The store emits a [38;5;153mchange[39m event on
  every mutation, and the persisted envelope carries a [38;5;153mversion[39m field. Those two things are the entire reason to care
  about architecture at this stage — they're what let the reminder engine attach as a subscriber and add its own
  per-task state by migration, rather than forcing a rewrite of the thing we're about to build. If you disagree with
  anything, that's the section where disagreeing is cheapest.

  Two things I deliberately left as open assumptions rather than pretending to know: that full re-render is fine at
  realistic list sizes, and that end-of-local-day is the right default due time for a date-only input. The second one
  becomes observable the moment notifications exist, so sub-project 2 will settle it.

  No code written yet — that starts after you approve the spec and I turn it into a plan.

